Hull-Drift

Creative Catalyst: The genre was born when the crew of the Icarus-V generation ship repurposed their ship's navigational rhythm to stabilize their decaying mental health mid-voyage.

Hull-Drift sounds like a sweltering 1950s Chicago dive bar transported into a pressurized titanium vacuum; it replaces the swing of Skas-horns with the icy, metallic screech of ship-vent oscillators and trades Bossa Nova’s beach warmth for the claustrophobic, humming acoustics of deep-space quarters.

Genre DNA
*   Tempo & Pulse: 115-130 BPM. A syncopated "stutter-skank" rhythm that mimics the erratic cycling of life-support air scrubbers.
*   Harmonic Palette: Diminished chord clusters, heavy use of the Locrian mode to mirror the feeling of being "off-course," and dissonance-laden major sevenths that refuse to resolve.
   Production Signatures: 1) Atmospheric Pressure Compression: Sound levels are modulated by the ambient vacuum density of the room; 2) Radiation-Jitter: High-frequency synth-glitches layered over analog percussion; 3) Hull-Resonance Reverb:* Feeding recordings back through the actual ship-hull vibration plates.
*   Dynamics: Tracks begin with isolated, rhythmic metallic thuds (the "Hull-Beat"), swelling into dense, chaotic horn-blares before collapsing into icy, spacious silences.

Scene & Origin Lore
Hull-Drift emerged in the "Belly"—the lower maintenance decks of the Icarus-V—where the ship’s third generation built a subculture based on "The Long Wait." They wear recycled pressure-suit nylon, patchworked with conductive copper wire, and host "Galley Shakes," ritualistic dance events where the gravity is intentionally cycled from 0.8g to 1.2g to force the music into the dancers' marrow. It is a music of profound grief and mechanical ingenuity, performed in flickering ultraviolet light, where the horn players use brass salvaged from the emergency life-raft couplings.

Instruments: Modified slide-guitar pickups mounted to titanium piping, analog air-flow synth modules (repurposed environmental controls), rhythm-driven shaker boxes filled with simulated lunar dust, and detuned tenor saxophones.

For Fans Of: Tom Waits, The Specials, Stan Getz, Sun Ra.

Fictional Band Name: The Oxygen Tax

Band Description: A trio of "ship-born" orphans, they are perpetually smeared with grease and wear polarized welding goggles. Their frontman, a lanky slide-guitarist named Kael, plays an instrument he calls the "Bulkhead-Slider," a long steel bar that emits whale-like moans, while his bandmates provide the jagged, syncopated rhythm section that turns every song into a panicked heartbeat.

